31,2,,status,May,,ALIVE,"{""seat"":1,""role"":""PARTICIPANT"",""alive"":true}"
32,2,,status,Minato,,ALIVE,"{""seat"":2,""role"":""PARTICIPANT"",""alive"":true}"
33,2,,status,Shizue,,ALIVE,"{""seat"":3,""role"":""PARTICIPANT"",""alive"":true}"
34,2,,status,Shunichi,,ALIVE,"{""seat"":4,""role"":""PARTICIPANT"",""alive"":true}"
35,2,,status,Victoria,,ALIVE,"{""seat"":5,""role"":""PARTICIPANT"",""alive"":true}"
36,2,1,talk,Minato,,"I found out that only two firms are doing the big digging work, and they are worried about clay and slippage. Also, the depot says we can only close the southern part in stages.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
37,2,1,talk,May,,"Oh, clay and slippage? Sounds like a messy start, @Minato! As for me, the numbers are a bit scary. Planted areas cost triple what hard surfaces do to maintain, and that gap is only growing.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""Minato""],""raw_differs"":false}"
38,2,1,talk,Shizue,,My findings bring a little sadness to my heart. It seems some of our past efforts have faded quickly; half the trees on the eastern avenue perished because they were not watered. We must be very careful.,"{""words"":37,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
39,2,1,talk,Victoria,,"My findings are equally sobering. The groundskeeping roll has shrunk from eighteen posts to just seven. While we have some bought-in services and a casual pool, our internal capacity for daily care is severely diminished.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
40,2,1,talk,Shunichi,,"It seems we are all discovering that the park is in a state of quiet crisis. My data shows people are treating the site as a shortcut to the tram, avoiding the margins entirely.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
41,2,2,talk,Minato,,"@Shunichi, it's sad that people only use the park as a shortcut. I'm worried about the clay and slippage the firms mentioned. If the ground is tricky, will it take a long time to finish?","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""Shunichi""],""raw_differs"":false}"
42,2,2,talk,May,,"@Minato, it might! But honestly, the real nightmare is what happens after the digging stops. If we plant too much to make it look pretty, we're just signing a blank check for the city. We can't afford a garden that eats the budget!","{""words"":43,""mentions"":[""Minato""],""raw_differs"":false}"
43,2,2,talk,Shizue,,"@May, you are quite right to be cautious. It is a tragedy when a beautiful vision is lost because we forgot the simple act of watering. We must ensure the care is promised, not just the planting.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[""May""],""raw_differs"":false}"
44,2,2,talk,Victoria,,"@Shizue, you have touched upon the core of the issue. With only seven staff members remaining on the roll, the ""simple act of watering"" becomes a systemic failure. We cannot assume the labor exists to support an ambitious design.","{""words"":39,""mentions"":[""Shizue""],""raw_differs"":false}"
45,2,2,talk,Shunichi,,"@Victoria, exactly. And while we're discussing labor, let's not forget the physics. My records show drainage callouts have doubled and the park is a heat trap. We can't just plant our way out of a drainage crisis.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[""Victoria""],""raw_differs"":false}"
46,2,3,talk,Minato,,"I didn't know the park was a heat trap, @Shunichi. That sounds uncomfortable for everyone. I'm just worried that if the ground is slippery and the firms are few, the work might drift and take forever.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[""Shunichi""],""raw_differs"":false}"
47,2,3,talk,May,,"@Minato, a long construction is a headache, but a permanent budget leak is a disaster! If we're already struggling with staff and costs, we can't just hope for the best. We need a plan that's actually sustainable.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[""Minato""],""raw_differs"":false}"
48,2,3,talk,Shizue,,"@May, your concern for the city's future is very thoughtful. It seems we are all seeing a pattern of things that look lovely at first but struggle to survive the passing years. We must be gentle and realistic.","{""words"":38,""mentions"":[""May""],""raw_differs"":false}"
49,2,3,talk,Victoria,,"@Shizue, being gentle is not enough. We must be honest about our limitations. If the staff roll has collapsed by over sixty percent, any plan requiring intensive manual upkeep is a fantasy. We need a design that respects our actual manpower.","{""words"":41,""mentions"":[""Shizue""],""raw_differs"":false}"
50,2,3,talk,Shunichi,,"@Victoria, you're finally speaking my language. Between the staffing collapse and the drainage failures, we're looking at a park that's practically fighting its own existence. We need a plan that accepts the tram-commuters and the heat, not one that ignores them.","{""words"":41,""mentions"":[""Victoria""],""raw_differs"":false}"
51,2,4,talk,Minato,,"I hope we can find a plan that is kind to the workers and the ground. If we pick something that isn't too hard to build, maybe it won't take so long! I'm ready for the candidates.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
52,2,4,talk,May,,"I'm ready too! Let's just hope the candidates aren't all ""dream gardens"" that will bankrupt us in three years. I'm looking for something sleek, simple, and—most importantly—cheap to keep up! Let's see the options!","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
53,2,4,talk,Shizue,,I am ready as well. I hope we find a plan that values longevity over a fleeting first impression. Let us look for a design that will be loved and tended to for many years.,"{""words"":35,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
54,2,4,talk,Victoria,,I am also ready. I will be looking for a candidate that acknowledges our staffing reality. We cannot award a plan that relies on a workforce that no longer exists. Let us see the proposals.,"{""words"":35,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
55,2,4,talk,Shunichi,,"I'm ready. Let's see if any of these architects have actually stepped foot in the park, or if they've just been staring at a glossy map in a climate-controlled office. I'm expecting a fight.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
56,3,,status,May,,ALIVE,"{""seat"":1,""role"":""PARTICIPANT"",""alive"":true}"
57,3,,status,Minato,,ALIVE,"{""seat"":2,""role"":""PARTICIPANT"",""alive"":true}"
58,3,,status,Shizue,,ALIVE,"{""seat"":3,""role"":""PARTICIPANT"",""alive"":true}"
59,3,,status,Shunichi,,ALIVE,"{""seat"":4,""role"":""PARTICIPANT"",""alive"":true}"
60,3,,status,Victoria,,ALIVE,"{""seat"":5,""role"":""PARTICIPANT"",""alive"":true}"
61,3,1,talk,Minato,,"The Woodland Walk sounds very pretty, but I'm worried about the clay and slippage. If we do a lot of digging for ponds in the Wetland Restoration, will it take too long to finish?","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
62,3,1,talk,May,,"@Minato, forget the digging for a second! Look at the Woodland Walk—dense trees and gravel? That's a maintenance nightmare. We'd be throwing money into a hole. The Market Plaza is much more my speed: paved, sleek, and affordable!","{""words"":38,""mentions"":[""Minato""],""raw_differs"":false}"
63,3,1,talk,Shizue,,"@May, I understand your wish for simplicity, but a paved plaza may feel cold to the heart. However, I worry for the Woodland Walk; without a promise of care, those trees may suffer as the others did.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[""May""],""raw_differs"":false}"
64,3,1,talk,Victoria,,"@Shizue, you are correct. With only seven staff members, the Woodland Walk is an impossibility. Similarly, the Wetland Restoration requires specialized care we cannot provide. The Market Plaza is the only option that respects our diminished manpower.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[""Shizue""],""raw_differs"":false}"
65,3,1,talk,Shunichi,,"@Victoria, for once, we agree. The Woodland Walk is a fantasy, and the Wetland Restoration is just a fancy way to invite more drainage callouts. The Market Plaza actually accepts the reality of the tram-commuters' foot traffic.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[""Victoria""],""raw_differs"":false}"
66,3,2,talk,Minato,,"@Victoria, @Shunichi, you both seem to like the Market Plaza. But I'm worried about the main gate. The depot said we can't hoard the center or the gate, so will the plaza work make the park too crowded?","{""words"":38,""mentions"":[""Victoria"",""Shunichi""],""raw_differs"":false}"
67,3,2,talk,May,,"@Minato, a little crowding during construction is a tiny price to pay for a park that doesn't bankrupt us! The Market Plaza is paved, which means low upkeep and no ""watering tragedies."" It's the only smart choice here!","{""words"":38,""mentions"":[""Minato""],""raw_differs"":false}"
68,3,2,talk,Shizue,,"@Minato, your concern for the people's comfort is very sweet. While the Market Plaza is practical, I wonder if it provides the peace a park should offer. Still, I fear the other plans are too fragile for our current care.","{""words"":40,""mentions"":[""Minato""],""raw_differs"":false}"
69,3,2,talk,Victoria,,"@Shizue, peace is a luxury we cannot afford if the park falls into disrepair. A neglected woodland or a stagnant wetland is far more distressing than a paved plaza. We must prioritize a plan that can actually be managed.","{""words"":39,""mentions"":[""Shizue""],""raw_differs"":false}"
70,3,2,talk,Shunichi,,"@Minato, the ""crowding"" is a temporary annoyance; a failed drainage system is a permanent disaster. The Market Plaza is the only plan that doesn't fight the people's habit of sticking to firm ground. It's the only rational choice.","{""words"":38,""mentions"":[""Minato""],""raw_differs"":false}"
71,3,3,talk,Minato,,"I understand. I just want to make sure we don't pick something that takes forever to build because of the clay. If the Market Plaza doesn't need deep digging, then I think it's a good idea.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
72,3,3,talk,May,,"@Minato, it's mostly paving and utility lines—way less risky than digging ponds or planting a forest! It's the only plan that doesn't look like a financial disaster waiting to happen. I'm totally sold on the Market Plaza!","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[""Minato""],""raw_differs"":false}"
73,3,3,talk,Shizue,,"@Minato, it is a comfort to know you are thinking of the workers. While my heart longs for the green, I cannot ignore the sadness of the dead trees. A plaza is a sturdy choice that will not break our spirits.","{""words"":41,""mentions"":[""Minato""],""raw_differs"":false}"
74,3,3,talk,Victoria,,"@Shizue, exactly. We must choose the path that ensures the park remains a functional asset rather than a burden. The Market Plaza is the only candidate that aligns with our actual staffing capacity. I vote for the Market Plaza.","{""words"":39,""mentions"":[""Shizue""],""raw_differs"":false}"
75,3,3,talk,Shunichi,,"@Victoria, you've hit the nail on the head. The other options are just glossy brochures for a world that doesn't exist. The Market Plaza is the only one that doesn't ignore the heat, the drainage, or the commuters. I vote for the Market Plaza.","{""words"":44,""mentions"":[""Victoria""],""raw_differs"":false}"
76,3,4,talk,Minato,,"I agree with everyone. If the Market Plaza is easier to build and doesn't need deep digging in the clay, it's the safest choice for the workers and the city. I vote for the Market Plaza!","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
77,3,4,talk,May,,"I'm with you all! It's the only plan that doesn't look like a budget-eating monster in disguise. Let's go with the one that's sleek, sustainable, and actually affordable. I vote for the Market Plaza!","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
78,3,4,talk,Shizue,,"It is a rare and beautiful thing to find such unity. While I will miss the greenery, I cannot let my heart blind me to the reality of our care. I vote for the Market Plaza.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
79,3,4,talk,Victoria,,"I am at peace with this consensus. By choosing the Market Plaza, we ensure the park is managed with dignity rather than falling into neglect. I cast my final vote for the Market Plaza.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
80,3,4,talk,Shunichi,,"It is almost refreshing to see us all finally surrender to the obvious. We've avoided the ""dream gardens"" and the drainage traps in favor of something that actually works with the ground and the people. I vote for the Market Plaza.","{""words"":41,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
